WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.142
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.142 --> 00:00:05.350
RAJEN SHETH: Hello, everyone.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.991
Thank you all for joining.

00:00:08.991 --> 00:00:10.740
We're going to talk a
lot about Chrome OS.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.140
And what our strategy
is, particularly

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.210
for businesses and
for enterprise,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.460
what we're doing with Chrome OS.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.080
And so my name is
Rajen Sheth, and I'm

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.710
the director of product
management for Chrome

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.720
for business and education.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.462
And we've been doing
a variety of things

00:00:26.462 --> 00:00:28.170
over the course of
the last several years

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.130
with Chrome OS, particularly
oriented around education,

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.610
but now really starting to
focus a lot more on enterprise,

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.460
and we have some very,
very interesting places

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.620
where we think this is
applicable in enterprise.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.160
So before we start off, I want
to kind of start with a story.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.200
So February 22 was the
10-year anniversary

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.600
of G Suite, which
used to be called

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.230
Google Apps in the enterprise.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.980
And I was the first
product manager for G Suite

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.170
and remember that
day quite well,

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.460
because we launched our first
kind of for-pay product out

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.620
of Google that was
not an Ads product.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.770
And it was interesting to
think back to that time,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.130
because one of the first
things that we did was we

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.080
went on a tour of
customers and we

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.160
talked to CIOs of a variety
of types about G Suite

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.190
and about the
cloud at that time.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:27.330
And almost universally, we
were thrown out the door.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.310
And I particularly remember
one customer where we went in,

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.770
five minutes into the meeting
they started to realize what

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.500
I was talking to them
about is taking their email

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.480
and moving it to our servers.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.510
And so he clarified and said,
are you asking me really

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.390
to take my email and
move it to your servers?

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.230
And I said, yeah.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.230
And he said, all right.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:48.229
I think we're done here.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:52.740
And so he gets up, leaves
the room, and that was it.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.190
And it's amazing to see,
though, how that has changed

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.510
over that course of time.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.790
Something that seemed crazy--

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.620
being able to put your
data in the cloud--

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.540
is now the mainstream.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.460
It's why all of us are here.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.430
What's interesting,
though, is that we really

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.650
think we're only
at the cusp of that

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.660
and there's much more
that can be done,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.870
and so much else has also
changed, with mobility,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:16.840
with devices.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.100
You think about how we thought
about devices back then.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.960
All of us probably
had one laptop

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.830
and that was all we used
for everything that we did.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.990
Now the world has
changed dramatically,

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.850
and it gives us
many opportunities

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.510
to kind of rethink the world
and rethink what we do.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.900
So the biggest opportunity
here is for businesses

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.390
to become data-centric.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:45.250
And this is both a challenge
as well as an opportunity.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.830
Almost every business
has data, of course.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.950
And businesses have been
data-centric, to an extent,

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:51.850
for a long time.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.590
But what really
changes right now

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:57.610
is how rapidly you can take
in the data, how rapidly you

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.030
can process that data, and how
rapidly you can do something

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.520
about it and actually change
your business as a result.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.160
And so what we
want to help with,

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.130
with the combination of Chrome
devices and Android and Google

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.850
Cloud, is to really help
businesses become data-centric.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.970
And what I mean by that is
kind of this flow right here.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:19.390
This is our connected
workspaces virtuous cycle.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.240
And where it all starts
is instrumenting.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.040
First, as instrumenting
your business

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:30.160
with devices, with spaces,
with a variety of places,

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.320
such that you're
bringing data in.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.600
The second thing is
drawing better insights

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.190
out of that data.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.650
And then the third thing
is actually making changes

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:43.060
with it, either via people
actually looking at the data

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.580
and making those changes,
or via machines actually

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:49.060
making those changes
automatically in their stead.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.000
And this is really the
opportunity that we have.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.720
So imagine if every
organization out there

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.570
had all parts of its
business connected

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:02.800
to the cloud at all times.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.570
And when I say "all
parts," I don't just

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.695
mean the knowledge workers
that you would typically

00:04:06.695 --> 00:04:08.770
think of that are carrying
around smartphones

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:09.820
and computers.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.170
I mean every worker.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.940
And I don't mean
just every worker.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.400
I mean all of their
customers as well--

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.470
people that might be
walking into a store, people

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.630
that they might be
interacting with.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.310
And also, I don't just
mean the customers,

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.860
I mean all of the spaces.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.220
So whether it's a retail store
or a manufacturing shop floor

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.820
or whatever it might be, what
if every thing there was also

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.170
connected to the cloud?

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.630
Imagine the kinds of
things that you can do.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.620
I'll give you an
example of this.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:42.640
So in a retail store, you think
about why we go to a store

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:43.480
right now.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.720
You know, I buy a
lot of things online,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.610
but the reason I
typically go to a store

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.640
is because I want to be able
to experience the product.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.979
I want to be able to see it
and touch it and feel it.

00:04:53.979 --> 00:04:56.020
I want to be able to browse
and find other things

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.670
that I might be interested in.

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:00.130
I might want to be able
to leverage the expertise

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:01.507
of the people in the store.

00:05:01.507 --> 00:05:03.340
And then I want that
immediate gratification

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.470
of being able to say,
I want that item,

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.390
and then be able to just
walk out with it and buy it.

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:12.280
And so those are all great,
but the things that are missing

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.790
are all the things that you
get when you go online and buy.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.489
When I go online and shop,
I can find something very,

00:05:18.489 --> 00:05:19.030
very quickly.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.030
I can find things that
are related to the things

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:21.910
that I like.

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:23.930
It knows me, and
so it knows what

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.739
I like and is able to actually
tell me more about it.

00:05:27.739 --> 00:05:29.530
And then I can see
reviews about that item,

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.510
to see what other
people thought about it.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.670
When I walk into
a physical store,

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.850
typically I don't
get any of that.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:39.550
And so how do we make it
such that a space like that

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.880
is able to be better?

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:45.820
So for example, I should be
able to walk into a store

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.830
and have a screen
that greets me,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:50.590
to be able to tell me where
in the store are things

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:51.910
that I'm interested in--

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.150
basically, based on either
something that I need to get,

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.040
or something that
I'm interested in.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.839
When I find something, I should
be able to ask the store clerk,

00:05:59.839 --> 00:06:02.380
and they should be knowledgeable
enough to tell me everything

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:05.990
that I need to know
about that item.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:08.620
I should be able to see
more information about that,

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.586
in terms of reviews,
what other people think

00:06:10.586 --> 00:06:11.710
about it, things like that.

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:13.420
And then I should be able
to purchase it right then

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.700
and there, as quickly and
seamlessly as possible,

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:16.960
and walk out.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.690
That's the kind of experience
that really transforms that.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:23.780
And we want to do that
from multiple industries.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.310
So the reality of the world
is much, much different.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.930
So first of all, there are
about 3.25 billion workers

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.570
that walk into a workplace
of one type or another

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:36.250
around the world right now.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.720
But there are only 750 million
PCs in business right now,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.340
and there are only
about 300 million

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.810
managed smartphones
out there right now.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.161
And so there's a
large number of people

00:06:48.161 --> 00:06:49.660
that when they are
in the workplace,

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.160
they are not connected
to the cloud.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:52.360
They don't have devices.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.490
They can't take advantage
of the kinds of things

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:55.744
that I just talked about.

00:06:55.744 --> 00:06:57.160
And there are a
lot of spaces that

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.990
are also just not connected.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:03.200
So how do we actually
make that a reality?

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.620
So we believe that with
Chrome OS and with Android,

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.650
we have the right tools to be
able to make that a reality.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.390
So both Chrome OS
and Android were

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.690
developed in the age
of the connected world.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.510
It was developed with
the internet pervasively

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.800
out there, with mobile
internet out there everywhere.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.650
And as a result of
that, we built this

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:28.850
with the concept of connectivity
inherently built into it.

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:31.210
We've also built these
operating systems

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:34.130
to be extensible across many,
many, many different types

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.550
of devices, everything from
a very large digital sign,

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:42.470
all the way down to a
sensor or a sensor hub

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:46.140
or the internet of things,
and everything in between.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.740
And so as a result
of that, we can now

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.080
start to actually
instrument and actually

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.920
get devices into many more
places and to many more people.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:58.070
And if you look at just the
devices that go to people,

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:03.290
we have OEMs by the thousands
that are actually manufacturing

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.160
these kinds of devices.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.240
And so we can really start to
address that gap of people that

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.780
don't have access to devices.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.570
And with Chrome in
particular, we're

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:17.060
really focusing
on two big things

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.510
to try to make this better.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:21.710
The first is to redefine
the concept of productivity,

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:25.160
and the second is the concept
of connected workspaces.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.910
And so I'm going to go into
these in a lot more detail

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.110
and we're going to show
you some examples of what

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.010
we're talking about here.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.802
So when we think
about productivity,

00:08:34.802 --> 00:08:36.260
the way I would
think about this is

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.809
that we usually, in the past,
have thought about productivity

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:42.380
as a laptop and a set
of tools on that laptop

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.290
to let you be able
to create documents

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.600
or create presentations
or create spreadsheets,

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.130
things like that.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.460
What we now need to really
think about is, if we zoom out,

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.080
what is productivity, really?

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.100
Especially if you have a device
in the hands of every employee

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:58.940
out there.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:01.430
Productivity for, for
example, a field technician,

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:04.070
is going to be a very
different type of thing

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:07.610
than productivity for somebody
that's sitting in an office.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:12.900
Productivity for a salesperson
is also a very different thing.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.140
So how do we rethink of the
concept of productivity?

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.560
The other thing, though, is
that we're getting to a world

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.800
where mobility is in the
hands of almost everybody.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:25.160
Almost everyone carries a
smartphone in their pocket.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:28.430
And so part one is, how can we
enable those smartphones to do

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:32.150
great things for these workers?

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.210
But then part two of
that is then, how do we

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:36.740
enable devices that are bigger--

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.410
tablets, laptops,
and other things

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:41.540
that are connected to that
mobile environment, that

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.750
run the same applications,
that are continuous

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.612
from that environment, as
opposed to discontinuous

00:09:47.612 --> 00:09:49.070
from that environment,
a completely

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:50.250
different environment?

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.440
And so that's what
we've been really

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:52.690
thinking about with Chrome OS.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.070
How can Chrome OS be
the right productivity

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:58.440
device for the mobile world?

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.820
So one of the big challenges
that people typically face

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:06.807
is, which device should
you choose at what times?

00:10:06.807 --> 00:10:08.640
A lot of times, people
don't have the luxury

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.460
of having all of these devices.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:11.240
They may have one.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.820
They may have two of these.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.670
And you have to be
switching back and forth

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.180
between all of these.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.540
What we're trying
to figure out is,

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:22.804
can we do it such that
you don't have to choose,

00:10:22.804 --> 00:10:25.220
and you don't have to switch
back and forth between these,

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:27.440
and we can make the
device that can actually

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.554
be versatile enough to
be all of these things?

00:10:30.554 --> 00:10:31.970
And then the second
thing is then,

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:34.220
how do we tie all of
these things together?

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:36.560
And so that's really what
we've thought about as we've

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.580
designed Chrome OS.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.390
So the first thing we did is
we took a step back to say,

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.240
if we redefined the
PC, what would it be?

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.340
What would be the
characteristics of a PC

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.350
if we had to reinvent it?

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.350
And we came up with this list.

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:51.920
One, it should be fast.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.750
You want to be able
to get in and get

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:57.357
to what you want to do as
quickly as you possibly can.

00:10:57.357 --> 00:10:58.940
The second thing is
it should be easy.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:02.510
So any user should be
able to start using it

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:06.050
without any training, and
be able to get in and do

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:08.330
what they want to do.

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:12.300
And that lets you scale to
users of many different types.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.246
The third thing is that
it should be secure.

00:11:14.246 --> 00:11:17.480
Security is obviously a
very, very difficult thing

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:18.050
with devices.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.540
But especially if you want
to have more and more devices

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:22.830
out there, security
becomes really critical.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.970
So how do we take the security
model and turn it on its head

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:28.880
and do interesting
things with it,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:32.240
so that you ensure, just out
of the box and inherently,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.370
people are secure
on their computers?

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.756
One other aspect that
was critical here,

00:11:36.756 --> 00:11:38.630
that we didn't realize
would be this critical

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:42.890
when we first started this
project, but has been crucial

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:47.240
in a variety of areas,
is shareability.

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:50.780
With a Chromebook, I can pick
up any Chromebook, log in to it,

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.350
and within seconds, I
have everything I need.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:54.260
I have all my data.

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:55.610
I have all my applications.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:56.970
I have even all my preferences.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:12:02.120
Even down to the wallpaper,
everything is the same.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:04.310
And then I could put that
away, go to another one,

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:07.040
pick it up, and
do the same thing.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.056
In a world where everybody
has their own PC,

00:12:09.056 --> 00:12:10.680
that doesn't really
matter a whole lot.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.720
But what we're seeing
is a shift there.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:16.400
And especially in a world where
people want different devices

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:19.430
at different times, you may not
want to arm every single person

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.650
with all devices at all times.

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.900
You may want to have it
such that people can pick up

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:25.880
the right device
whenever they need it,

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:29.510
and be able to do what
they want with it.

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:32.000
One example of this is,
again, in retail stores.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.740
We've seen this
happen quite a bit,

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:37.400
where they may have 50
sales associates that

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.430
may go through that retail
store on a given day,

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.200
but they may have
only room to be

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.450
able to do two or three devices.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.665
But if they want to move all
of their processes online,

00:12:47.665 --> 00:12:49.040
those 50 people
are going to have

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:51.440
to share those two or three
devices very, very well.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:55.220
And Chrome has that
built in, inherently,

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:57.020
as one of its things.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:01.370
A couple of last things is that
these devices should get better

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:02.570
over time.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:06.620
Typically, when you buy a
device, or when you buy a PC,

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.020
over time it gets worse
and worse and worse

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:12.440
and more bloated and
slower, and eventually you

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:14.960
need to then just replace
it with something else.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.540
We wanted to make it such
that these get actually

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:20.250
better over time, and
they don't get worse.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:23.090
And the last thing is that
we want it to be versatile,

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:24.770
so that it should
be able to shift

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.870
between different
types of use cases,

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:29.660
and shift very naturally.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.770
So on that last point
about versatility,

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:34.270
there are two parts of it.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.880
One is kind of the
continuity with what people

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.010
are doing elsewhere, and
then the second thing

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:40.360
is the device itself.

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:41.860
So in terms of
continuity, we wanted

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:44.740
to ensure that the way
people are interacting

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:47.140
with their smartphone is
also how they can interact

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:49.157
with their PC, and
then they can start

00:13:49.157 --> 00:13:50.740
interacting with it
in a different way

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:53.560
as well, when they need
kind of full productivity,

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.290
multiple windows,
multi-processing,

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:57.650
things like that.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:14:00.590
And so there are a few areas
that we looked at with that.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:02.980
The first is the device itself.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.240
So the device--
originally, Chromebooks

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:08.800
have been laptop devices.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:10.720
And what we've
now done is really

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:13.690
focused on making
them more versatile,

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.680
and making them
convertible devices,

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:19.060
and eventually, things like
detachable devices and tablets

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.610
and things like
that as well too.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:24.070
So the examples you
see here are a couple

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:26.680
of the new devices we released--
the Samsung Chromebook

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:30.550
Pro and the ASUS
Chromebook Flip,

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.320
where you can flip
these devices over

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:34.810
and you're able to interact
with them like a tablet,

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:38.170
and actually interact with
them very well as a tablet.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:41.340
A couple of small things that we
did to really make this great,

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:44.800
so the Samsung device
is a 3 by 2 ratio,

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:48.222
whereas most PC screens
are a 16 by 9 ratio.

00:14:48.222 --> 00:14:49.930
And we did that because
we knew that when

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.930
you want to go into a
tablet mode, a 3 by 2 ratio

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:53.980
is actually a lot better.

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:57.430
If I want to flip it on its
side and look at it like a book

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:00.150
or like a piece of
paper, a 3 by 2 ratio

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:02.330
is the right thing to do.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:06.250
So to that degree, we wanted
to make it a great tablet.

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:08.020
But then beyond that,
too, another thing

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:11.830
that we see is very important
is writing and stylus.

00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:14.260
And so we built styluses
into many of these devices,

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:17.200
so you can now write
on this device.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:20.170
So I can use this device,
in an average business day,

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:23.230
as my fully-productive
primary laptop.

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:25.120
I can use it as a
tablet, when I want

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:28.810
to flip through a presentation
or when I want to read a book

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.050
or when I want to read a
paper or whatever it might be.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:35.560
And I can use it as my notebook
to take notes in a meeting.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.170
And so you can use
it as all three

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:41.620
of these, kind of all at once.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:46.900
But anybody can do that,
in terms of the hardware.

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:50.500
What really makes this
work well is the software.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:54.060
And so if you think about
convertibles or detachables

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.520
of other types that
you see in the market,

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:58.690
there's a fundamental
problem you run into,

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:03.300
which is you may have a
convertible or detachable

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.640
that's a wonderful PC,
but then when you flip it

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:09.040
into tablet mode, you don't
have the applications there

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:12.310
to be able to make
it a great tablet.

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:14.934
Or you might have it such
that it has a great mobile app

00:16:14.934 --> 00:16:17.350
ecosystem, but then when you
start to do productive things

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:19.420
and you want to have
multiple screens

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:22.370
and you want to have kind
of that multitasking,

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:23.810
it's not very good at that.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:26.609
We need it to be good
at both, and so one

00:16:26.609 --> 00:16:28.150
of the things we
introduced last year

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:30.784
is the idea of Android apps
running on Chromebooks.

00:16:30.784 --> 00:16:32.950
And we're rolling that out
to all of the Chromebooks

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:36.940
that are being sold
in the market in 2017.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:38.560
And now what you can
do is essentially

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.650
run the entire Google Play
Store on the Chromebook

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:45.600
and be able to leverage any of
the 2 million applications that

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:46.370
are there.

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:49.690
And so it gives you that kind
of flexibility and versatility

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:51.700
from an application perspective.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:54.610
You still get the productivity
of a Chromebook-- you know,

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:58.840
multiple-sized window apps,
world-class desktop browser,

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:02.560
multitasking, access to desktop
virtualization for your Windows

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.922
apps, all of those
kinds of things,

00:17:05.922 --> 00:17:07.630
and the manageability
that comes with it.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:09.010
But then in addition
to that, you

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:12.160
get all of these
touch-optimized applications.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:13.492
And you have that consistency.

00:17:13.492 --> 00:17:15.700
So if you're using an
application on your smartphone,

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:20.980
you can also use that same
application on your laptop

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:22.990
now as well, too.

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:25.660
And so this is something
that lets us kind of then

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.640
really span from the
phone all the way

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:31.480
up through large device.

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.610
And the last part
of that is really

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:35.800
kind of the access to the
different types of apps

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:36.440
that you have.

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:41.200
And so now we support HTML5,
and we support Google Play.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:43.390
But we've also done
interesting things

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:44.860
to integrate in Windows apps.

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:47.740
And so for example, we've
worked with folks like Citrix

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:50.380
to make it such that a
Windows app can actually

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:53.690
be launched from our launcher,
just like any other app.

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:56.350
So you'll see an icon for
that app in our launcher.

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:59.564
You click on that app and it
opens it in its own window.

00:17:59.564 --> 00:18:01.480
If you didn't know
anything, you wouldn't even

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.620
know that that was actually
an app that wasn't resident

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:06.710
on that device.

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:08.620
And so that way, you
can go to even the apps

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:11.560
that you were using on other
platforms as well, too.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:18.270
So that's kind of how we're
working on mobile productivity.

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:21.520
You're going to see many more
things that we're doing there.

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:23.670
But the other part
of this is this idea

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:25.860
of connected workspaces.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:27.230
How do we take a space--

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:30.570
and like I was saying, a retail
store, a manufacturing shop

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.360
floor, a variety of
spaces, an office space--

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.030
and really make that
space connected,

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:40.170
and combine the power of Chrome
and Android with Google Cloud

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.090
to do really
interesting things, in

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:45.120
particular, vertical solutions?

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:47.400
And we think that
by doing this, we

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:49.905
can actually
transform these spaces

00:18:49.905 --> 00:18:51.710
and actually create value.

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:53.790
So in the retail
store, make it such

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.530
that people end up selling more.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:58.295
In a manufacturing
shop floor, make

00:18:58.295 --> 00:19:01.190
it so it's a much more
efficient environment

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.320
as a result of this, and so take
a very verticalized approach

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.660
to this as well, too.

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:09.550
So we talked about this
a little bit before,

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:12.660
but if you look at how
people think of data insights

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:15.060
and actions right now
and how they should be,

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:16.330
it is a little bit different.

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:19.950
So data, we're in an
environment right now,

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:23.100
in many cases, where data
is either non-existent or is

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:26.850
based on poor sources
or is really incomplete.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:29.130
And in addition to that, a
lot of the data coming in

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:31.920
might not be stored in
a centralized place.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:33.450
So how do we get
it such that it's

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:37.590
gathered from many different
sources, from more sources,

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.240
and stored centrally?

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.280
The second part of
this is the insights

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:45.311
that you get from them are
oftentimes very subjective.

00:19:45.311 --> 00:19:47.310
So how can you make it
such that you're actually

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:53.070
drawing useful
feedback from this data

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:54.840
and actually getting
the right insights?

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.131
And how do you get it such
that you're drawing insights

00:19:57.131 --> 00:20:00.420
that a human may not have
found, if they're looking

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:03.460
at that same piece of data?

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:08.460
And then helping enterprises
and organizations by doing that.

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.380
The last part is
then the actions.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.870
A lot of times actions
are very slow to roll out.

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:16.160
How can you make the actions,
in some cases, immediate, such

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:19.860
that the systems themselves
can learn from themselves

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:24.590
and get better and
better over time?

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.710
So we think that with the
combination of Android

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:31.660
and Chrome and Google
Cloud Platform and G Suite,

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:33.340
we can actually
start to do this,

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:35.140
because with Android
and Chrome, you

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:38.500
can start to get more devices
of many different types

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.890
out there to different
environments,

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:46.270
and as a result of that,
more data coming in with it.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:48.250
You can also have a
great security model

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.080
by which you can actually
secure and manage

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.360
these devices in
a consistent way,

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:58.780
such that you're able to
increase the number of devices

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:01.810
without increasing the
number of IT people

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:03.220
that you need for that.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.320
The second part is
Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:08.290
Using things like BigQuery
and machine learning,

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:11.290
you can start to draw insights
out of this immediately,

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:14.050
and start to draw those kinds
of insights, like I was saying,

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:16.665
that you may not see
with the human eye.

00:21:16.665 --> 00:21:18.040
And then the last
part of this is

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:20.740
you can connect it to the
productivity tools like G

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.320
Suite, such that humans can
actually take a look at this

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:26.920
and be able to gather their
own insights out of this.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:30.160
Finally, we can connect it to
a variety of other things going

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:34.000
on within Google that make
this story even better-- things

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.370
like Firebase, to make it
so that you can very quickly

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:39.880
and easily develop mobile
applications, things

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:44.170
like DoubleClick, such that you
can be able to push content out

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:46.690
to some of these
devices very easily,

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:49.520
especially things that are
in customer-oriented spaces,

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:51.820
things like progressive
web apps and Polymer that

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:55.210
make it easy to build very,
very solid applications

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:57.580
on the front end, and then
things like Google Assistant,

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:01.540
eventually, which can
make it so that users

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:06.050
can start to get insights
from this as well, too.

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:10.170
And so if we look at
that virtuous cycle

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:11.970
that I talked about
at the beginning,

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:14.820
you can start by instrumenting
things with the devices

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:15.930
that we talked about.

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:19.020
You can then analyze them
with the tools of Google Cloud

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:19.590
Platform.

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:22.400
And then you can improve
them using that data

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:25.500
and using apps built
on top of that that can

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:29.660
change automatically with this.

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:34.760
And we offer many of
the parts that are here.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:37.640
But then the other
part is that this

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:39.740
offers an opportunity
for partners

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:42.290
to be able to actually build
on top of this as well,

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:44.150
and build things
that are specific

00:22:44.150 --> 00:22:45.830
for particular verticals.

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:48.290
And you're going to hear
about one of these solutions

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:53.240
in a little bit, where it opens
up a set of APIs by which you

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:55.670
can do this and actually
connect together

00:22:55.670 --> 00:23:00.690
all of the various applications
that are there within Google.

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:03.240
So let me give you
an example of this.

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.360
All of us have
gone into a store,

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.820
and when we first
go into a store,

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.170
we see a bunch of
different signs

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:12.420
that are there with the store.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.600
Many of these signs are paper.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:19.440
We've now gotten to the
point where with Chrome, we

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.270
can make it as inexpensive to
put up a digital sign as it

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:26.760
is to put up a paper sign, and
in some cases, less expensive,

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:29.850
because the paper sign you have
to keep changing over time.

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:32.054
A digital sign, you
don't have to do that.

00:23:32.054 --> 00:23:34.470
One of the things we have, we
released a couple years ago,

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:38.340
was an $85 dongle called
the Chromebit, which

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:40.770
you can stick into a monitor
and turn that monitor

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:44.970
into a smartscreen, basically
turn any screen that you

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:46.590
have into a digital sign.

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:48.600
So we're now starting
to see retailers

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:51.090
start to use it in
really interesting ways

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:56.640
to have up-to-date
information on their sign.

00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:58.890
We're starting to
see, though, others

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:01.320
use this in a very
interesting way

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:03.270
to be able to do
even more than that

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:06.370
and actually gather
insights from that sign

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:12.300
and be able to actually
change things as they go on.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:13.800
And so I'm going
to play this video.

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:15.480
This is from Coca-Cola.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:19.170
Coca-Cola has been doing some
phenomenal things with Chrome

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:23.820
to be able to really generate
more sales in grocery

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:26.280
stores of Coke products
and other products

